There's a lot of advice out there about brewing coffee, and a lot of gear to go with it. Most of it doesn't matter nearly as much as a few simple things. If you get those right, you can make a really good cup with a cheap pour-over cone and a kettle.

Good beans are the starting point (that's what [Understanding Coffee Beans](/coffee/guides/understanding-coffee-beans/) is about). This series is about not wasting them once you get them home.

This is the overview. Each of the four below has its own guide with the details.

## 1. Ratio: how much coffee to how much water

This is the one that makes the biggest difference for the least effort. Too little coffee and it's weak and sour, too much and you are wasting good beans. A ratio like **17:1** (17 grams of water for every gram of coffee) is an easy place to start for pour-over, and it's easy to adjust from there.

I weigh my coffee and water instead of scooping. A scale is cheap, and it takes the guessing out of it and it makes the ratios easy for different amount of coffee.

**Read more:** [Coffee-to-Water Ratios](/coffee/guides/coffee-to-water-ratio/), with starting ratios by method and a little calculator.

## 2. Grind size: how fine or coarse

Grind size controls how fast the water pulls flavor out of the coffee. Finer grinds extract faster, coarser grinds extract slower, so each brew method has a range it likes. Espresso is fine, French press is coarse, and pour-over sits in the middle.

This is also where a good grinder earns its keep. An even grind brews evenly.

**Read more:** [Grind Size](/coffee/guides/coffee-grind-size/), with a chart from fine to coarse by method.

## 3. Water and temperature

Coffee is almost all water, so the water matters. Filtered water is an easy improvement. Temperature matters too, and it's worth playing with, especially with lighter roasts.

**Read more:** [Water and Temperature](/coffee/guides/coffee-water-and-temperature/).

## 4. Dialing in by taste

Once the basics are close, your tongue does the rest. Sour usually means the coffee is under-extracted, bitter usually means it's over-extracted, and there's a short list of things to change for each.

**Read more:** [Dialing In by Taste](/coffee/guides/dialing-in-coffee-by-taste/), with a cheat sheet for what to adjust.

## Where to start

If you only change one thing, start weighing your coffee and water. If you change two, add [grinding fresh](/coffee/reviews/hand-coffee-grinders/). Those two together will make more difference than almost any new brewer.

And change one thing at a time. If you change the grind, the ratio and the water all at once, you won't know which one helped.
